WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Having comprehension questions doesn't
make it, you know, doesn't enhance

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.880
it as language learning material.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.810
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here
today, and today I wanna talk

00:00:09.815 --> 00:00:12.330
about comprehension questions.

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.930
You know, after you read something
or after you listen to something,

00:00:15.935 --> 00:00:20.280
very often we're asked to, you know,
explain or answer questions about

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:21.810
what we have listened to and read.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.040
So comprehension questions today
is my subject, if you would, if you

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.840
enjoy my videos, please subscribe.

00:00:28.244 --> 00:00:31.965
Click on the bell for notifications,
and if you follow me on a podcast

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:33.405
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:33.405 --> 00:00:34.305
I do appreciate it.

00:00:34.665 --> 00:00:38.385
So comprehension questions
are kind of a mainstay of

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:40.035
traditional language instruction.

00:00:40.364 --> 00:00:44.025
We have a story, then we're asked
questions about that story to

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:45.675
prove that we understood the story.

00:00:46.394 --> 00:00:50.085
Um, I never do comprehension questions.

00:00:50.235 --> 00:00:51.614
I hate comprehension.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:57.780
Uh, there's a famous quote from
Rubem Alvez, a very well known

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:02.190
Brazilian educator, where he says,
being asked questions about what we

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:08.130
have read is sort of the surest way
to destroy any enthusiasm readers

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.710
have for the activity of reading.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.120
And, and the reason for that is simple.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.990
If we're learning a language and
we listen to a story, we aren't

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.580
going to remember everything.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.919
We aren't going to understand everything.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:23.370
It's still an extremely valuable activity.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.080
Uh, it's possible to enjoy a story
and not understand everything.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.820
It's, IM, it's possible to listen to a
story and enjoy the story or an item of

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:38.220
content and misunderstand fair amount of
it, or miss certain parts of it, and still

00:01:38.225 --> 00:01:43.410
feel that we are sort of experiencing the
language, getting closer to the language,

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.200
grabbing bits and pieces of meaning.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:52.200
And the thought that we have to then
try to remember what happened, uh,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:57.660
where there's a good possibility that
we won't remember or we'll get it wrong.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:03.555
I find that to be an extremely sort of
negative or destructive type of activity.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.190
It's fine to say, ask people,
did you like the story?

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:09.870
What did you like about the story?

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:11.550
Or What did you like about the content?

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:17.100
Talk to me a little bit about the
content as a means of, of, uh,

00:02:17.105 --> 00:02:20.910
inducing the person to speak if
in fact that's what we wanna do.

00:02:20.915 --> 00:02:26.010
I'm not tremendously keen on the idea of
forcing learners to speak either, but I

00:02:26.015 --> 00:02:30.030
understand that in a classroom, that's one
of the things that teachers have to do.

00:02:30.299 --> 00:02:32.549
But then just let people talk at random.

00:02:32.549 --> 00:02:36.660
Say something about what you just
listened to, uh, or what you just read.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:37.859
That's good enough.

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:43.245
And if we don't have those comprehension
questions so much, the better.

00:02:43.575 --> 00:02:46.725
Uh, you know, I have mentioned before
that the Canadian government created

00:02:46.725 --> 00:02:53.144
this language learning, uh, website
called Mauril, M A U R I L, Mauril maybe.

00:02:53.595 --> 00:02:57.615
Uh, which of course is not exactly
what people search for when they're

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:01.215
looking for a language learning
app, but it is also based on the

00:03:01.215 --> 00:03:03.345
idea of comprehension questions.

00:03:03.345 --> 00:03:08.475
So they, you know, programs from Radio,
Radio Canada or CBC and they prepare a

00:03:08.475 --> 00:03:13.725
bunch of comprehension questions for the,
this content, which in their mind converts

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.305
it into language learning material.

00:03:16.605 --> 00:03:19.454
But it was already language
learning material by the mere

00:03:19.454 --> 00:03:22.125
fact that you had audio and text.

00:03:22.245 --> 00:03:26.535
That is language learning material,
having comprehension questions doesn't

00:03:26.535 --> 00:03:30.165
make it, you know, doesn't enhance
it as language learning material.

00:03:30.614 --> 00:03:38.730
Um, so I, I would say, If the goal is
to enjoy listening, read, then simply

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:41.070
refuse to do comprehension questions.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:42.540
I refuse all questions.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.609
I don't do grammar questions.

00:03:44.609 --> 00:03:46.530
I don't do any kind of drill.

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:52.140
To me engaging with content,
hopefully content of interest to

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:57.090
as much of it as possible where you
can hear it, hopefully understand

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:58.380
a lot of what you're listening to.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.469
Then read it kind of mine the
material for words and phrases.

00:04:02.745 --> 00:04:07.484
Go and listen again, hopefully understand
a little bit more, probably not a lot more

00:04:07.484 --> 00:04:09.885
unfortunately, but that's the process.

00:04:10.155 --> 00:04:14.625
And scratching your brain to
try to remember what happened in

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:19.725
whatever you listen to, uh, is
not helpful to language learning.

00:04:20.175 --> 00:04:25.305
Um, so refuse to do
comprehension questions.

00:04:25.425 --> 00:04:26.835
That's my advice.

00:04:26.985 --> 00:04:31.705
Focus instead on enjoying the
content that you're listening to.

00:04:31.735 --> 00:04:36.405
You'll advance more quickly, you'll
enjoy the process much more, and

00:04:36.405 --> 00:04:40.935
you'll improve your listening
comprehension, uh, far more quickly.

00:04:40.965 --> 00:04:46.755
Now, I know that a lot of the sort of
tests of English as a foreign language,

00:04:46.875 --> 00:04:50.955
for example, TOEFL or TOEIC, are
based on the, or a large part of those

00:04:50.955 --> 00:04:54.855
tests consist of, uh, showing that
you understood what you read or you

00:04:54.855 --> 00:04:56.745
understood what you were listening to.

00:04:57.105 --> 00:05:01.050
So from that point of view, Uh, sure.

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:05.940
Spend a little bit of time going
over previous TOEIC uh, tests.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.050
Example tests, sample tests,
or similarly for TOEFL.

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:14.460
But if you want to be good
at understanding what was in

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.570
the story, you have to expose
yourself to a lot of content.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:20.220
You have to read a lot.

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:21.630
You have to become a good reader.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:23.159
You have to become a good listener.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:29.505
And you do, you do that not by answering
questions about, you know, content

00:05:29.505 --> 00:05:31.034
items that you've listened to or read.

00:05:31.155 --> 00:05:34.955
You do that by becoming more and
more familiar with the language,

00:05:35.865 --> 00:05:40.605
acquiring a larger and larger
vocabulary so that the language is

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:45.255
not an obstacle, but you listen to it
with a high degree of comprehension.

00:05:45.435 --> 00:05:49.965
And if that's the case, when you
need to, answering comprehension

00:05:49.969 --> 00:05:54.825
questions for a TOEIC or a TOEFL exam
becomes a lot easier if at an, if

00:05:54.825 --> 00:06:00.120
at an early stage you are constantly
having to explain what you read.

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:04.770
That's destroying the fun of reading,
in my opinion, or constantly having to

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:09.300
explain what you just listened to, that
it destroys the pleasure of listening.

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:12.960
You want to build up momentum
in your listening and reading

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:15.060
so that you enjoy that activity.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.790
The brain will naturally become
more and more familiar with

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:19.500
the language, and if at a...

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:24.520
you're, you know, going to be faced with
a TOEFL exam, a TOEIC exam, an IELTS

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:31.270
exam by all means do some sample exams,
but if you've proceeded without constant

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:35.340
questioning, comprehension questions
and so forth, you will do better.

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:39.510
And if you aren't faced with the
prospect of having to write some kind

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:43.290
of a language, uh, exam, if you're
learning a language simply out of

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:48.690
interest or for pleasure for sure,
unless you like doing them, avoid,

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:50.659
avoid the comprehension questions.

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:51.940
Okay.

00:06:51.969 --> 00:06:52.664
There you have it.

00:06:52.664 --> 00:06:55.365
I will leave a couple of, uh,
videos that I've done in the past

00:06:55.365 --> 00:06:56.925
on this subject just for reference.

00:06:57.465 --> 00:06:58.425
Thank you for listening.

00:06:58.695 --> 00:06:59.294
Bye for now.

